  <section>
    <h2>What changes with the heat source?</h2>
    <p>This comparison covers conventional electric stone heaters, rather than infrared cabins. Both an electric stone heater and the P1 heat the room and sauna stones. Both can produce löyly when water is added to the hot stones according to the heater’s instructions.</p>
    <p>Choosing pellets adds a fire you can watch and a fuel supply you manage. It does not, by itself, guarantee better steam or a more comfortable sauna. Heater sizing, stone capacity, ventilation, and room design also matter.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Installation and everyday use</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Factor</th>
          <th scope="col">Electric stone heater</th>
          <th scope="col">P1 pellet stove</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Power</th>
          <td>Many models need a dedicated circuit; check the model’s wiring requirements.</td>
          <td>Designed for a standard 110V outlet. An existing outlet and circuit must be suitable.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Installation</th>
          <td>No combustion exhaust. Requires heater clearances and sauna ventilation.</td>
          <td>Through-wall fit, exterior hopper access, exhaust venting, clearances, floor protection, and ventilation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Controls</th>
          <td>Temperature controls; remote start available with some models and control systems.</td>
          <td>Designed with phone-based start and temperature control.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Fuel</th>
          <td>No stored fuel.</td>
          <td>Buy pellets, keep them dry, and refill the hopper.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Upkeep</th>
          <td>No combustion ash. Stones and heater still need inspection and maintenance.</td>
          <td>Ash removal and stove and exhaust maintenance, as well as care of the stones.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For examples of electric heater wiring, stone care, and steam use, see the <a href="https://resources.saunaplace.com/pdfs/Harvia-Spirit-IOM.pdf">Harvia Spirit manual</a>. The <a href="https://nepis.epa.gov/Exe/ZyPURL.cgi?Dockey=P101069T.txt">EPA’s pellet stove guide</a> covers general fuel and maintenance considerations; P1 installation and operating requirements must come from its own documentation.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Compare the whole cost</h2>
    <p>Start with installation quotes. Electric may involve a new circuit or panel work. The P1 may involve a wall opening, vent components, floor protection, and installation labor. The less expensive option depends on what your sauna already has.</p>
    <p>For running costs, compare electricity used per session multiplied by your electricity rate with pellets used per session multiplied by your pellet price per pound. Include the P1’s electrical use too. Count warm-up as well as bathing time, and compare similar room temperatures and session lengths.</p>
    <p>A lower price per bag or per kilowatt-hour won’t tell you which heater costs less to run. You need measured fuel and electricity use for comparable sessions. Local energy prices, insulation, weather, and how long you run the sauna all affect the result.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>When electric is a good fit</h2>
    <p>If you want to avoid buying fuel and handling ash, electric is worth considering. It may also be easier to install where suitable wiring already exists or where a combustion exhaust route is impractical. Remote control is available on some electric systems, so that feature alone does not decide the choice.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>When to consider the P1</h2>
    <p>The P1 is worth considering if you want a wood fire without adding logs during a session, have room for its through-wall installation, and are comfortable storing pellets and maintaining a combustion appliance. An existing suitable household outlet may also let you avoid adding a dedicated electric-heater circuit.</p>
    <p>Its published sauna range is 210–500 cubic feet. Check the <a href="/p1-pellet-sauna-stove#specifications">current specifications</a> and <a href="/no-electrician-sauna-heater">installation planning guide</a> against your room. The P1 is in pre-order with certification pending, so confirm final installation requirements before planning construction around it.</p>
  </section>
